# Line endings are pinned to LF, everywhere, in the working tree as well as in the repository.
#
# This is not tidiness. Two things here read and write FILE BYTES rather than git objects, and
# both are silently wrong on a CRLF checkout:
#
# - `tools/deploy.sh` uploads the working tree to the live site and verifies each file by
#   comparing the server's copy against THAT SAME LOCAL FILE. A whole-tree CRLF conversion is
#   therefore invisible to it: every file would be reported "uploaded and verified" while the
#   site received bytes nobody intended to ship.
#
# - `tests/mutations.php` finds its target text by exact string match, and its targets are
#   written with `\n`. On a CRLF checkout every MULTI-LINE mutation matches nothing. The harness
#   is honest about it -- it reports BROKEN rather than SURVIVED, which is the whole reason that
#   rule exists -- but the effect is that most of the suite's proof of falsifiability quietly
#   stops running on that machine. Measured on a Windows machine, 2026-08-14: single-line
#   mutations killed their checks, multi-line ones all reported `target text occurs 0 times`.
#
# Git for Windows ships `core.autocrlf=true` in its system config, so a fresh clone there gets a
# CRLF tree unless this file says otherwise. That is the default case, not the unusual one.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Vendored third-party source is exempt, and stays byte-for-byte as upstream shipped it.
#
# PhotoSwipe arrives with CRLF and with trailing whitespace on some lines. Normalising it would
# rewrite ~4,700 lines of somebody else's code for no benefit -- nothing here reads those files
# by line, and a vendored copy that no longer matches the release it was taken from cannot be
# diffed against the next one. It also makes `git diff --check` report the upstream trailing
# whitespace as newly introduced, which invites "fixing" a third-party file.
assets/vendor/** -text

# Anything whose bytes are the content. `text=auto` guesses well and does not need to guess.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.zip binary

# A compiled gettext catalogue is a binary format with its own internal offsets; a line-ending
# conversion does not merely reformat it, it corrupts it.
*.mo binary
